    {
      "dom_id": "question-94606",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=(z-2)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{8} & =(0-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{16}{8} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-6 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =6\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(4,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
